WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.152 --> 00:00:07.334
It has been said that "History is written by the victors."

2
00:00:08.054 --> 00:00:12.595
In other words, it's those who win battles, not those who lose them, who end up influencing history.

3
00:00:13.376 --> 00:00:20.037
William the Conqueror was definitely victorious at the Battle of Hastings in 1066, when he took control of

4
00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:21.378
Anglo-Saxon England.

5
00:00:22.098 --> 00:00:26.559
Although he lived a thousand years ago, his influence can still be felt today in many parts of

6
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:27.039
the world.

7
00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:32.514
"History is written by the victors" also means that the victor's version of events is usually the one

8
00:00:32.554 --> 00:00:33.375
that is remembered.

9
00:00:33.995 --> 00:00:39.719
However, the Normans' version of their conquest of England was not written down on paper; it was embroidered

10
00:00:39.799 --> 00:00:43.001
on a long piece of cloth known as the Bayeux Tapestry.

11
00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:49.205
Created about thirty years after the invasion, it features over seventy different scenes with Latin captions.

12
00:00:50.006 --> 00:00:55.129
In fact, it looks like a kind of medieval comic strip, and remains a crucial source of information

13
00:00:55.189 --> 00:00:56.530
about these long-ago events.

14
00:00:57.591 --> 00:00:58.592
So who was William?

15
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.122
This Norman is considered to be one of the greatest soldiers and rulers of the Middle Ages.

16
00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:07.825
The word "Norman" comes from the word "Norsemen," meaning "people of the north."

17
00:01:08.446 --> 00:01:16.830
Excellent sailors and ferocious fighters, the Norsemen—also known as Vikings—came from Norway, Sweden, Denmark, and Iceland.

18
00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:23.554
In 913, some Norsemen invaded northern France and settled in an area that became known as Normandy.

19
00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:28.829
Although they pledged loyalty to the French king, they remained very independent and powerful.

20
00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.733
In 1035, William's father died, and the seven-year-old boy became duke of Normandy.

21
00:01:35.373 --> 00:01:40.216
But William was illegitimate, and as a result, many people did not want him to rule.

22
00:01:41.056 --> 00:01:46.259
Conflicts broke out, and three of William's guardians died violently—even his tutor was murdered.

23
00:01:46.919 --> 00:01:51.142
But William managed to survive, and these early challenges helped form his character.

24
00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:55.204
He was intelligent, strong, energetic, determined, and a fierce soldier.

25
00:01:56.308 --> 00:02:00.771
The Bayeux Tapestry's first scene, however, shows not William, but his cousin, Edward.

26
00:02:01.412 --> 00:02:05.815
Edward's grandmother had married into the English ruling family, and Edward became king.

27
00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:10.858
He had no children, and according to the tapestry, had promised to make William his heir.

28
00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:13.640
But William faced serious competition.

29
00:02:14.281 --> 00:02:21.245
Two powerful Anglo-Saxon noblemen, Harold and his brother Tostig, wanted the throne; so did the king of Norway.

30
00:02:22.046 --> 00:02:28.619
Before Edward died in January 1066, he chose Harold as his successor, and Harold was crowned king.

31
00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:34.543
According to the tapestry, however, Harold had sworn an oath to support William's claim to the throne.

32
00:02:35.284 --> 00:02:38.246
William felt deeply betrayed and decided to invade England.

33
00:02:39.326 --> 00:02:44.330
William hoped to sail to England in August, 1066, but the winds were not good for crossing the

34
00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.552
English Channel, and his ships had to wait.

35
00:02:47.192 --> 00:02:50.334
In early September, the Normans were still waiting for favorable winds.

36
00:02:51.075 --> 00:02:56.440
Unfortunately for Harold, most of his southern army consisted of peasants who needed to return home to harvest

37
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.020
their crops.

38
00:02:57.781 --> 00:03:00.183
So on September 8, he reluctantly let them go.

39
00:03:00.904 --> 00:03:02.645
But Harold's troubles were just beginning.

40
00:03:03.386 --> 00:03:07.549
Tostig and the king of Norway were now invading in the north of England, and he was forced

41
00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.271
to gather more troops to confront them.

42
00:03:10.092 --> 00:03:15.196
On September 25, Harold defeated both his opponents, and he and his men began to march back to

43
00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:15.697
the south.

44
00:03:16.537 --> 00:03:22.311
Back in France, the winds had finally changed, and on September 28, the Normans landed near Hastings, on

45
00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:23.632
England's southeast coast.

46
00:03:24.873 --> 00:03:31.399
About two weeks later, Harold finally reached Hastings, his foot soldiers exhausted from fighting and marching.

47
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:38.865
William's troops were well rested, and his army included archers and about two thousand cavalry in addition to

48
00:03:38.926 --> 00:03:45.872
infantry. On the morning of October 14, before the English could properly prepare for battle, the Normans attacked.

49
00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.903
The English soldiers defended themselves by forming a shield wall; they lined up side by side in rows

50
00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:53.644
and locked their shields together.

51
00:03:53.664 --> 00:03:59.628
At first, the Normans could not break through this wall, and they suffered many casualties due to the

52
00:03:59.748 --> 00:04:01.749
Anglo-Saxon's two-handed battle axes.

53
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.832
Soon, the Norman cavalry panicked and fled.

54
00:04:06.432 --> 00:04:11.455
Rumors flew that William had been killed, so William removed his helmet to show he was alive.

55
00:04:12.135 --> 00:04:17.258
Inspired, his men turned back to the battlefield and were able to destroy the English troops, who had

56
00:04:17.278 --> 00:04:19.640
broken formation to follow the retreating Normans.

57
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.943
According to the Bayeux Tapestry, by nightfall, Harold was dead (the result of an arrow to his eye),

58
00:04:26.544 --> 00:04:30.145
his troops were in disarray, and the Normans had won the Battle of Hastings.

59
00:04:31.166 --> 00:04:36.769
William was crowned king of England on Christmas Day, 1066, and began taking land away from the English

60
00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:37.910
to give to Norman lords.

61
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.672
Many Anglo-Saxons rebelled, especially in the north.

62
00:04:42.592 --> 00:04:47.776
The Normans slew them by the hundreds and destroyed farms and villages, even killing livestock so that any

63
00:04:47.816 --> 00:04:49.097
survivors starved to death.

64
00:04:50.037 --> 00:04:55.141
With the English finally subdued, William set about establishing a system of rule known as the feudal system.

65
00:04:55.961 --> 00:05:00.704
He granted land and power to certain lords, who swore loyalty to the king and agreed to supply

66
00:05:00.745 --> 00:05:02.145
him with soldiers in wartime.

67
00:05:02.906 --> 00:05:05.988
The lords, in return, leased parts of their lands to their knights.

68
00:05:06.869 --> 00:05:11.152
At the bottom of this social system were the peasants, who worked the land and served those above

69
00:05:11.192 --> 00:05:16.503
them. The Anglo-Saxons had never built castles, but the Norman nobles built them everywhere.

70
00:05:17.404 --> 00:05:23.268
From within these castles, they governed their subjects and organized armies, and the castles became very visible reminders

71
00:05:23.328 --> 00:05:25.530
of Norman power and the feudal system.

72
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:32.374
Modern historians know much about the period immediately following the invasion from the Domesday Book, a survey of

73
00:05:32.474 --> 00:05:34.256
England conducted in 1086.

74
00:05:35.176 --> 00:05:42.614
Written on sheepskin parchment, the book describes 13,418 places, most of which, amazingly, can be found on maps

75
00:05:42.674 --> 00:05:49.339
today. For each place, the book records details about the people living there, the buildings, the land, the

76
00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:50.460
farm animals, and more.

77
00:05:51.221 --> 00:05:53.282
Even the numbers of wild horses are listed.

78
00:05:54.043 --> 00:05:59.447
Originally compiled so William knew how much each plot of land should be taxed, this remarkable book gives

79
00:05:59.527 --> 00:06:02.110
us a "snapshot" of England in the eleventh century.

80
00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.993
It remains one of England's most significant historical documents.

81
00:06:06.773 --> 00:06:09.836
William also made changes to the administration of justice in England.

82
00:06:10.496 --> 00:06:16.098
Before the Norman conquest, laws and customs could vary from village to village and from decade to decade.

83
00:06:16.858 --> 00:06:22.099
William centralized the legal system with a royal court, the King's Court, and sent out judges all over

84
00:06:22.119 --> 00:06:23.900
the land to uphold the country's laws.

85
00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.241
This system became known as "common law" because it applied to everyone in the same way.

86
00:06:29.982 --> 00:06:33.863
To this day, democracies base their judicial systems on common law.

87
00:06:34.943 --> 00:06:39.728
Last but not least, the English language as it is spoken today is a direct result of the

88
00:06:39.788 --> 00:06:40.609
Norman invasion.

89
00:06:41.489 --> 00:06:47.611
The Norman nobles spoke French and Latin, and while they did not replace Anglo-Saxon English, they certainly changed

90
00:06:47.711 --> 00:06:54.312
it. Under Norman influence, English became more flexible, acquiring thousands of new words from the Norman tongues.

91
00:06:55.113 --> 00:07:00.794
The Normans prove that history is literally written by the victors, since historians have only the documents left

92
00:07:00.854 --> 00:07:03.175
by the conquerors of the Anglo-Saxons.

93
00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:10.485
Many people argue that these accounts are not accurate and not fair to the Anglo-Saxons, but sadly, they

94
00:07:10.525 --> 00:07:15.812
may never know Harold's side of the story since he, unlike the victors, left no records behind.
